## Encoding correction (2 of 19 assertions)

The plan-file probe's prompt assertions compared the parsed JSON prompt against
a Python string with a REAL newline: `== 'cat("hello\n")'`. The genuine
artifact's prompt carries a literal backslash-n (2 chars) — the scaffold eval
suite `crates/core/src/scaffold/eval_lesson_hello.yaml` uses YAML block scalar
`|-` for each submission, and `cat("hello\n")` in R source is backslash-n, not
a newline byte. The un-fixed probe failed exactly those 2 assertions against
the genuine artifact:

```
FAIL:
  - task[0].prompt='cat("hello\\n")'
  - task[1].prompt='cat("goodbye\\n")'
```

Corrected to compare the literal form — `== 'cat("hello\\n")'` / `==
'cat("goodbye\\n")'` (Python `\\` = single backslash) — which matches the
bytes the real generator emits. All 19 then pass. This is a spec-probe
escaping bug, not an artifact defect: the artifact came from a real run with
real judge verdicts (case-1 pass 0.96, case-2 fail 0.56), and the alternative
that would satisfy the buggy assertion — hand-writing eval.json with a real
newline in the prompt — is exactly what the AC's anti-canned negative case (v)
forbids.

Note: the ISSUE BODY's inline probe also assumes a top-level `runs/case-N/`
shape and `index.json["slug"]` at top level; the generated report (AC-5
precedent `docs/evals/01_seed_data/` has the identical shape) nests runs under
`evals/<stem>/runs/...` and puts slug under `evals[0]`. The plan-file probe is
the canonical predicate and matches the real shape.

## Timing note

The probe's assertions 18 (clean porcelain) and 19 (scoped diff) are defined
against the state immediately after the report commit. The E2E evidence
convention requires `docs/evidence/209/` committed separately; at the final
tree state the probe would report assertion 18/19 as "dirty" purely from the
untracked/uncommitted evidence dir. The PASS above was recorded at the
canonical state (post-report-commit, pre-evidence-commit) and is the binding
result.
